Release 0.1.0
-------------

This is a pretty good version for resources evaluations and extended engine tests with many configurations.

People is encouraged to make tests with parameters other than 32 bits registers, 1 bit arithmetic size etc...

What is NOT included:


Angle gene does not follow the serial calculation schema.
The resources may be a little bit underestimated. The master clock is lower than it could have been.


The filters are not connected to the signal analysis.
This is an intermediary version to hard-code the shifts of the first order filters.
One is encouraged to modify the values (in the pre filter package file) and check if the results are coherent.


The folders schema and the Makefiles are not yet decided.
There are some standard way to organize the files, especially for ASIC.
This project is highly configurable and intended to build on many targets ASIC and FPGA.
There are tests at high level (VHDL) and low level (C++).
The FPGA version is going to build on many FPGA and some might have a sample draw of the PCB.
A huge reorganization is coming.


The RAM based prefilter (and light filter) is not tested at all.
Two of the 3 ways are tested, the barrel shifter and the direct access. The RAM based is not.
For an ASIC build, there are RAM generators that can build the relevant configuration and fit the direct access.
However, for FPGA, the resources are over estimated as the 2 tested solutions are consuming many logic elements, and leave the RAM idle.


The main filter has not yet be designed.
The main filter is currently badly simulated using a set of first order prefilters.
An idea is the resources sizes can be taken from many low pass filter implementation.
Despite of some analogue implementation using cascaded RC, this one "virtually" include a 1 gain isolation amplifier between the stages.
That means the curve is a little bit more close to the 2 asymptotes of the low pass filter.


Some extra modules are planned to test the project out of the shelf.
* A mantissa exponent converter.
  The goal is to limit the bandwidth with a pseudo logarithm output with less bits.
* An AGC. All the exponents of all the channels are going to be increased or decreased to allow a better display.
  In case of an overflow, all the exponents are decreased immediately.
  In case no channel reaches the highest value, all the exponents are increased very slowly.
* A Q&D VGA graphical output.
* Perhaps an Ethernet output. The data is going to be sent as basic proprietary packets of size lower than $8000.
  Linux and perhaps MacOS-X machines can read using the raw_data constant rather than the AF_INET, AF_INET6, ARP etc...
  while opening the port (and not associate IPv4 nor IPv6 configuration).
* A Q&D ADC handler.
* Some more clean (than the CXX verification one) waveform generators.
